### Security Checklist for Every Code Change

Before committing any code, verify:

- [ ] No secrets or API keys in code
- [ ] Authentication checks are in place
- [ ] Authorization verified (user owns resources)
- [ ] Input validation implemented
- [ ] Error handling doesn't leak information
- [ ] Sensitive data not logged
- [ ] HTTPS used for all API calls
- [ ] Dependencies reviewed for vulnerabilities
- [ ] Rate limiting considered (if applicable)
- [ ] Timeouts configured for external calls
- [ ] Security rules updated (if database changes)

### React Native Specific Security

#### Secure Storage
- Use `expo-secure-store` for sensitive data
- Never use `AsyncStorage` for secrets
- Use Keychain/Keystore for platform-specific secure storage

#### Network Security
- Always use HTTPS (never HTTP)
- Validate SSL certificates
- Use certificate pinning if high security needed
- Handle network errors gracefully

#### Permissions
- Request permissions explicitly
- Explain why permissions are needed
- Handle permission denials gracefully
- Revoke permissions when no longer needed

### Firebase Security

#### Firestore Security Rules
- Write rules for all collections
- Test security rules thoroughly
- Never allow public read/write on user data
- Use `request.auth` to verify identity
- Validate data structure in rules

#### Firebase Functions Security
- Always verify authentication
- Validate all inputs
- Use environment variables for secrets
- Implement proper error handling
- Monitor function execution logs

#### Firebase Storage Security
- Set appropriate security rules
- Validate file types and sizes
- Scan uploaded files for malware (if applicable)
- Use signed URLs for temporary access

### Performance Guidelines

#### Optimization
- Use `React.memo()` for expensive components
- Implement `useMemo()` for expensive computations
- Use `useCallback()` for stable function references
- Optimize images (compress, use appropriate formats)
- Implement code splitting for large features

#### Loading States
- Always show loading indicators for async operations
- Implement skeleton screens for better UX
- Use optimistic updates when appropriate

### Git Conventions

